# Line endings are pinned to LF, and this file is the only thing that decides.
#
# The machine this was written on has core.autocrlf=true, so without this file
# a checkout rewrites the working tree to CRLF, and a trailing \r turns every
# line of a shell script into a command that does not exist. The scripts run
# through sh on a user's machine are the ones that cannot survive it:
#
#   bin/tracedoc          run by Claude Code on every hook
#   build.sh              run by anyone rebuilding the shipped binaries
#
# Nothing else here depends on it, the Go sources least of all. The note that
# stood here claimed a CRLF checkout would change usage()'s bytes -- it is a
# raw string literal -- and so fail the staleness guard. That was wrong on
# three counts: the Go spec discards '\r' inside raw string literals, the
# staleness guard compares behaviour rather than bytes, and build.sh pins the
# build ID with -ldflags so no source byte can reach it. Corrected 2026-10-03.
#
# The explicit eol skips git's binary detection, so the shipped builds are
# marked -text by hand below. Without that, a checkout would rewrite every byte
# of them.
* text=auto eol=lf

bin/tracedoc-darwin-amd64 -text
bin/tracedoc-darwin-arm64 -text
bin/tracedoc-linux-amd64 -text
bin/tracedoc-linux-arm64 -text
bin/tracedoc-windows-amd64.exe -text
bin/tracedoc-windows-arm64.exe -text
